  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 141 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nHigh pressure aloft across the Desert Southwest will gradually \nweaken as deep troughing dives southeast from western Canada. \nSoutherly flow will support warmer temperatures with a return to \nupper 80s to lower 90s for Thursday and Friday. Broad upper-level \nheight falls from the approaching longwave trough will overspread \nthe area. Low pressure across the Northern Plains will drag a cold \nfront south into the area during the afternoon/evening hours. NBM \nQPF probabilities continue to favor areas to our south and east and \nas a result, the going forecast keep most of the forecast area dry. \nThat said, determinstic solutions vary slightly on placement of this \nboundary by roughly 100-200 miles. Any slower progression with this \nfeature will bode more favorable for local rain chances thus \nconfidence in precipitation potential remains low for now. More \nunsettled weather will be on tap for the weekend as enhanced mid-\nlevel flow resides immediately north of the area. Though upper-level \ndynamics will largely miss the immediate area, reasonable moisture \nfetch from the Gulf combined with mid-level moisture originating \nfrom the Eastern Pacific will combine to produce PWAT values nearly \n1-2 sigma above normal values for mid-September. NBM remains fairly \nnoisy with broad low-probabilities for wetting rain and this closely \naligns with the latest EPS solution. That said, deterministic \nECMWF/GFS solutions are fairly aggressive with rainfall over the \nweekend but particularly Sunday night as a surface low takes shape \nin the lee of the Central Rockies. This will be something to watch \nin the coming days including a potential severe threat with stronger \nmid-level flow supporting a sheared environment. Of course the \nquestion remains where the frontal boundary sets up. NBM inner-\nquartile spreads remain large, 20F for North Platte on Sunday, \nhighlighting this uncertainty.\n\nTowards the middle of next week, upper-level ridging will redevelop \nover the southern CONUS with increasing influence across Nebraska. \nThough NBM MaxT spread remains large, gradual day over day increases \nare noted from Monday through Thursday. While heights aloft will \nincrease, non-zero precipitation potential will exist. Though no one \nday appears to be a washout, recurring rain chances should be enough \nto keep us on the wetter side of normal for the time frame. This \naligns with the latest Climate Prediction Center 8-14 outlook which \nhedges towards > 40% probabilities of above normal precipitation for \nTuesday through the end of next week. Details are far from certain \nso continue to check back with later forecasts for details.",
